<h2>Product architecture</h2>
<p>The main design challenge is sharing deterministic music logic across two very different presentation platforms without forcing browser APIs, DOM assumptions, or native navigation into the common package.</p>
<pre class="mermaid">flowchart TD
  W[React and Vite web app] --> S[Shared TypeScript music core]
  M[Expo and React Native mobile] --> S
  W --> A[ASP.NET Core API]
  M --> A
  A --> AP[Application layer and contracts]
  AP --> D[Domain bounded contexts]
  A --> INF[Infrastructure and EF Core]
  INF --> DB[(PostgreSQL)]
</pre>
<p>Logical dependency diagram based on the repository structure; not a production deployment topology. The shared core is client-side reusable logic, not an intermediary service.</p>
<h2>Decisions visible in the implementation</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Backend:</strong> a .NET 10 modular monolith with separate API, Application, Domain and Infrastructure projects; bounded contexts include Identity, Practice and Billing.</li>
<li><strong>Platform-specific adapters:</strong> navigation, authentication session handling, native storage and audio playback remain outside shared TypeScript logic.</li>
<li><strong>Shared domain calculations:</strong> the <code>@praxis-studio/core</code> package contains music logic for fretboard displays, modes, backing tracks, chords, exercises and settings.</li>
<li><strong>Product workspaces:</strong> the web application distinguishes learning/creation activities from training/practice experiences, with routes and tested workspace-resolution logic.</li>
<li><strong>Quality:</strong> the web and mobile projects include automated unit/component tests and the web project provides end-to-end test configuration.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Tradeoff: shared logic versus server-owned capabilities</h2>
<p>Sharing calculation and content logic improves parity, but not all operations belong in a downloadable client bundle. The repository's engineering notes explicitly identify premium server-owned computations as a workstream. This page does not imply that those future security changes have all shipped.</p>
<h2>Evidence boundary</h2>
<p>The application repository is private. This case study summarizes inspected source and documented architectural decisions without linking proprietary files, showing secrets, or disclosing operational security details. A public UI screenshot is evidence of the product presentation—not proof of any backend guarantee.</p>
